WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.300
<v ->Next, we're getting into DDoS attacks.</v>

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.900
So DDoS stands for Distributed Denial of Service.

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.880
It's a way to basically shut down an instance

4
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.640
by making it sure it cannot accept any new traffic

5
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.190
effectively making it useless

6
00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.890
and so your users cannot access your application anymore.

7
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.080
So the way it works that you have an attacker

8
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.487
and you have the application server,

9
00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.740
and the attacker through some various techniques,

10
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.150
that we'll not get into,

11
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.670
will be able to have a bunch of computers

12
00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.110
that are going to be masters

13
00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:29.580
and they're going to create bots

14
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.320
and the bots will be a lot of them

15
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.740
and all the bots are going to get into requesting

16
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.890
stuff from our application server in very bad ways,

17
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.090
non-conventional ways,

18
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.190
and so the application server is overwhelmed,

19
00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.960
it doesn't know how to respond to this request.

20
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.640
It's going to try as best as it can,

21
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.930
but it's gonna be very soon overwhelmed

22
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.610
and then it will be just done

23
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.200
and so there will be a denial of service.

24
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.580
And so your normal users

25
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.980
cannot access your application server

26
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.430
because it does not respond anymore to normal requests.

27
00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.280
It's very dangerous, but it's very common on the internet.

28
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.570
And so there are different types of attacks

29
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:01.410
on your infrastructure.

30
00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.440
There is DDoS and so it's when your service isn't available

31
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.200
because it's receiving too many requests at a high level.

32
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.040
So there's different ones.

33
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.010
There's SYN Flood, which is a layer four type of attack

34
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.950
when there's too many TCP connection requests happening.

35
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.800
There's UDP Reflection where other servers

36
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.100
can send a big UDP request to our server.

37
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.070
DNS flood attack, where we overwhelm a DNS

38
00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.740
so that users cannot query our DNS service

39
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.410
and then can't find our website, which is bad.

40
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.650
Then the Slow Loris attack, where a lot of HTTP connections,

41
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.320
so it's layer seven, are opened and maintained

42
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.330
and our server is trying to maintain all of them.

43
00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:40.530
The threads are being overwhelmed and the server is useless.

44
00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.240
Then there's application level attacks

45
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.740
which is more complex, more specific

46
00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.400
and you need to understand how the target application works

47
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.920
to make it an application level attack.

48
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.870
And so before it was network based attack,

49
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.130
now it's application level.

50
00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.340
And so you have some things such as case bursting strategy

51
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.160
to overload the backend database with a request

52
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.480
to invalidate the cache

53
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.890
or you request too many things

54
00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.990
or you send too many big packets.

55
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.370
It just depends on your application

56
00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.440
and the kind of security holes

57
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.240
your developers may have introduced

58
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.920
while programming your application.

59
00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.890
Okay, so how do we get protected from DDoS

60
00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.650
and these application level attacks on AWS?

61
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:20.520
Well, first for DDoS, there is AWS Shield Standard,

62
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.500
and it's to protect against DDoS attacks

63
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.210
for your websites, applications,

64
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.430
for all customers at no additional cost.

65
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:29.490
So by using AWS, you are somewhat protected,

66
00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.950
thanks to Shield Standard enabled by default

67
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.750
against some DDoS attacks.

68
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.430
Then you have Shield Advanced,

69
00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.980
which gives you 24/7 premium DDoS protection

70
00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.150
and we'll see in details

71
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.010
what that means in the next slide.

72
00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.350
You have WAF or Web Application Firewall,

73
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.720
and this is not specifically for DDoS

74
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.230
but it can help you filter specific requests based on rules.

75
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.450
So for example, you can say if the request

76
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.320
is more than five megabytes, then drop it right away

77
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.120
because it looks like a bad request.

78
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.510
So WAF is more against application level protections

79
00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.830
but it can still help you

80
00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.780
filter some specific requests based on rules

81
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.390
and helps you survive a DDoS attack.

82
00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.310
We have CloudFront and Route 53

83
00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.290
which by default have Shield enabled

84
00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.030
and they will give you availability protection

85
00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.380
by using a global edge network.

86
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.370
So attacking CloudFront and Route 53 is a lot of work.

87
00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.090
And combined with Shield, it provides DDoS attack mitigation

88
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.800
directly at the edge of your network.

89
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.870
So the traffic never really makes it

90
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.890
through to your core application in one region.

91
00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.080
And finally, in case the traffic

92
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.030
does reach your applications,

93
00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.740
then a very good way to protect yourself

94
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.090
is to be ready to scale.

95
00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.070
So you need to leverage AWS Auto Scaling,

96
00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.500
such as if some of your servers are overwhelmed

97
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.950
that means your at least CPU is skyrocketing close to 100%,

98
00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.420
then Auto Scaling will kick in

99
00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.710
and create more instances

100
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.350
and so it's going to become more expensive for your attacker

101
00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.790
to attack these new instances.

102
00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.110
Obviously it's gonna be more expensive for you

103
00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.760
but you're having some kind of protection

104
00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:54.960
against these attacks.

105
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.330
And then finally, it's a very good idea

106
00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.100
to separate static resources

107
00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.170
and distribute them through S3 in CloudFront

108
00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.300
which is visually very very safe

109
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.840
and stable from a DDoS, from dynamic request

110
00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.880
which are going to be usually smaller requests

111
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:09.713
and that you can handle

112
00:04:09.713 --> 00:04:12.480
for your REST API on EC2 and ALB.

113
00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.730
If you're more interested into DDoS protection as a whole,

114
00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.550
I'd recommend this white paper right here.

115
00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.770
Okay, so as a sample architecture

116
00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.570
to protect yourself against DDoS,

117
00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.300
you have the DNS service Route 53

118
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.760
that is protected by Shield by default.

119
00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.160
Then you have your application distributed by CloudFront

120
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.900
where Shield is again enabled by default

121
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.300
and you can set up a Web Application Firewall

122
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.030
on your CloudFront distribution to protect yourself.

123
00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.370
Finally, if the traffic does make it through,

124
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.710
then on your load balancer you also have Shield.

125
00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.110
And then if your application makes you through

126
00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.520
to your Auto Scaling group,

127
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.290
then it's able to scale over there

128
00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:47.370
and make sure it can handle

129
00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.700
the extra bits of traffic from the DDoS attack.

130
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.170
Now let's get into Shield.

131
00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:55.350
So Shield has two level of service.

132
00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.640
Shield Standard is free

133
00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.580
and it's activated for every customer and ON by default.

134
00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.960
And it will provide you protection

135
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.440
from SYN attacks, UDP Floods, Reflection attacks

136
00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.440
and other layer three or four type of attacks.

137
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.610
Very very good.

138
00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.880
Shield Advanced is for more enterprise customers.

139
00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.040
It's an optional DDoS mitigation service.

140
00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.610
It's going to cost you $3,000 per month per organization

141
00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:18.750
and you're going to be protected

142
00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.790
against more sophisticated attacks

143
00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.828
on your EC2 instance, ELB, CloudFront,

144
00:05:23.828 --> 00:05:27.150
AWS Global Accelerator and Route 53.

145
00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.520
On top of it, if you do experience a DDoS,

146
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.140
then you have 24/7 access

147
00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.260
to the AWS DDoS response team, the DRP.

148
00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.840
And on top of it, if you incur higher fees

149
00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.360
because you are scaled up with Auto Scaling

150
00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.070
because you have more requests going into your network,

151
00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:42.300
more bandwidth,

152
00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.540
then you are protected against these higher fees,

153
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.310
thanks to the Shield Advanced

154
00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.020
sort of insurance that you took.

155
00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.010
So it's very important

156
00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.960
for you to understand the difference between these two

157
00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.510
and understand which one to use in the exam.

158
00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.100
So that's it for this lecture.

159
00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.050
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

